Would this imply that less parameterized attention mechanisms = less effective ICL? Less parameterized FFNs in the form of MoEs seem to not have problems with model knowledge store. What about if we were to stack 2 attention layers before the FF layer?
Quoting @Grad62304977
@rami_mmo @neurallambda Also I think transformers are beautiful when fully viewed in the query key value mechanism and should be taught more like that. Keys and values are ur knowledge store, in the self attn layers its data dependent and therefore ur in context memory, in the ffn its data independent therefore it’s ur model knowledge store. In the ffn u remove data dependency to then allow u to use a much larger key value store. I think that’s why I love rnn hybrids as ur doing a similar trade off and seems like its always worth it (esp newer rnns are getting much better)

Btw I’ve tried using this and as long as you normalize the “softmax” result again so it all adds up to 1, convergence shouldn’t be affected. I really wonder if it’ll help with the weirdness of some attention logits
